 diversity of marine life, including thousands of species of fish, corals, mollusks, and other organisms. This biodiversity makes it one of the most complex and important ecosystems on the planet. However, the reef faces significant threats from climate change, coral bleaching, pollution, and overfishing, prompting ongoing conservation efforts to protect and preserve its unique environment.\n\nThe Great Barrier Reef has received several significant recognitions. In 1981, it was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site, acknowledging its outstanding universal value and the need for its protection. CNN named it one of the Seven Natural Wonders of the World in 1997, emphasizing its global significance and natural beauty. In 2006, the Queensland National Trust declared it a state icon of Queensland, reflecting its cultural and environmental importance to the region."}}